Your heart doesn't wait for orders from your brain. A tiny patch of special cells in its upper right chamber fires an electrical signal about once a second. The signal spreads across the heart and makes it squeeze. Doctors call this patch the heart's natural [[@accident-pacemaker|pacemaker]].

Your brain can still speed it up or slow it down. When you run, feel scared or get excited, your heart beats faster to deliver more oxygen to your muscles.

{{story:blue-whale}}

A police or referee's whistle has a small ball inside, often made of cork or plastic, called a pea. As air swirls round the chamber, it bounces the pea around, which keeps interrupting the airflow. That makes the note warble, a trilling sound that's easy to hear over shouting crowds and traffic.

One of the best-known makers was Joseph Hudson, a toolmaker in Birmingham, England. His company, Acme, began making whistles in the 1870s. In the 1880s, London's Metropolitan Police chose his loud whistle to replace the wooden rattles constables had used to call for help. Acme still makes whistles used in football matches around the world.

{{story:whistled-languages}}

लेह के पूरब में चांगथांग पठार लगभग 4,000 से 5,000 मीटर की ऊँचाई पर है, यानी शिमला से लगभग दोगुनी ऊँचाई पर। हवा पतली है, आँधियाँ तेज़ हैं और बहुत कम कुछ उगता है। [[@people-nomads-india|घुमंतू चरवाहे]], चांगपा लोग, साल भर अपनी बकरियों, भेड़ों और याकों को लेकर एक चरागाह से दूसरे तक जाते हैं।

बकरियाँ छोटी और मज़बूत होती हैं, लंबे बाहरी बालों और मुड़े हुए सींगों वाली। उनका महीन भीतरी रोआँ ही उन्हें ज़िंदा रखता है। चरवाहे कहते हैं कि सर्दी जितनी कड़ी, रेशा उतना ही महीन और घना उगता है।

{{story:people-nomads-india}}

Braille is a system of raised dots that people read with their fingertips. It was invented in France by Louis Braille, who was blinded in an accident as a small child. He published his system in 1829, after developing it from the age of 15.

A refreshable braille display is a flat device with rows of tiny pins that pop up and down to form [[@braille|braille]] letters. Connected to a phone by Bluetooth, it lets a person read texts and emails with their fingers. These displays are expensive, often costing more than the phone itself, so groups in many countries, including India, have worked on cheaper versions.

{{story:computer-vision}}

Roll two dice and the total is not equally likely to be anything. There are 36 ways the dice can land, and 6 of them make 7. Only 1 makes 2, and only 1 makes 12. Board game designers use this. In the game Catan, the number 7 triggers a robber, because it comes up so often.

Cards work the same way. A pack has 52 cards, so the chance of drawing an ace is 4 in 52, or 1 in 13. Card players who remember what has been played are really updating fractions in their heads.

{{story:probability}}

People played chess at a distance long before computers. In correspondence chess, players posted one move at a time by letter, and a single game could take more than a year. In the 1990s, websites such as the Internet Chess Club let people play live over slow dial-up connections.

Chess.com started in 2007. Lichess, a free site run as a charity with no adverts, followed in 2010. Smartphones then put a chessboard in millions of pockets. Fast formats suit phones well: in bullet chess each player has just one minute for the whole game, so every move is a scramble.

{{story:ai-what}}

The first stage is meipayattu, which means body exercise. Students learn eight main stances named after animals: the elephant, lion, horse, boar, snake, cat, rooster and fish. Each has its own balance and power. The cat stance is low and springy. The horse stance is stable and grounded.

Then come leaps that turn into kicks above head height, splits and spins. A skilled fighter can jump, twist and land facing the other way. Teachers say a student's body should become "all eyes", sensing danger from every side. That needs lightning-fast [[@reflexes|reflexes]]. Traditionally, much training happened in the cool [[monsoon|monsoon]] months, when bodies were thought to be most supple. A session often ends with the student bowing to the teacher, called the gurukkal, and touching the floor of the kalari.

{{story:reflexes}}

For years, coaches thought the fastest runners simply moved their legs faster. In 2000, scientists in the USA tested runners on special treadmills that measured their footsteps.

They found that fast and slow runners swing their legs forward at almost the same speed. The difference was in the push. Faster runners hit the ground harder, so each step carried them further. At top speed, an elite sprinter's foot is on the ground for under a tenth of a second, and pushes with around four to five times their body weight.

{{story:body-speed-limit}}

धरती पर [[gravity|गुरुत्वाकर्षण]] खाने और पेय को पेट के निचले हिस्से में रखता है, और गैस ऊपर तैरकर निकलने को तैयार रहती है। अंतरिक्ष की कक्षा में ऊपर-नीचे कुछ होता ही नहीं। खाना, पानी और गैस, सब पेट के भीतर साथ-साथ तैरते रहते हैं।

इसलिए जब अंतरिक्ष यात्री डकार लेते हैं, तो गैस अक्सर खाना या पानी भी ऊपर ले आती है, जिसे गीली डकार कहते हैं। कुछ अंतरिक्ष यात्री इसी वजह से अंतरिक्ष स्टेशन पर सोडे वाले पेयों से बचते हैं। उनकी एक तरकीब है दीवार को धक्का देकर शरीर को आगे बढ़ाना, जिससे गैस पेट के एक सिरे की ओर खिसक जाती है।

{{story:space-body}}

The Greek historian Herodotus visited Egypt in the 400s BCE and described what he was told. First, priests drew out the brain through the nose with a metal hook. Then they cut open the left side of the body and removed the stomach, intestines, [[@liver|liver]] and lungs. These were dried and stored in four special containers called canopic jars. In later times, organs were wrapped and put back inside.

Next came the natron. This salt, collected from dry lake beds such as those at Wadi El Natrun, draws water out of the body, the same way [[@salt|salt]] dries fish or meat. [[bacteria|Bacteria]], which cause rotting, cannot live without water. It is the same science people use to [[@food-preserve|preserve food]].

{{story:food-preserve}}

The men of the *Dei Gratia* noticed some odd things. There was about a metre of water sloshing in the bottom of the hull. One of the pumps had been taken apart. A rod used to measure water in the hold lay on the deck. Clothes, the crew's belongings and the captain's furniture were still in place. Nine of the alcohol barrels were empty.

The *Dei Gratia* crew sailed the ship to Gibraltar and claimed a reward for saving it. A court looked hard at whether they had done something wrong, found no proof, and gave them about a sixth of the ship's value.

{{story:shipwrecks}}

Some cities have found clever fixes. Food banks in the United States collect unsold food from shops and give it to families who need it. In India, many gurdwaras and temples run community kitchens that feed thousands of people a day, planning carefully so little is left over.

Food that really cannot be eaten can still be useful. Composting turns peels and scraps into rich soil, with help from [[@earthworms|earthworms]] and [[microbe|microbes]].

{{story:earthworms}}

Cooking may have begun hundreds of thousands of years ago, after people learned to control [[@fire-discovery|fire]]. For most of history, a kitchen meant a hearth, a ring of stones or a clay stove.

{{story:fire-discovery}}

In India, the chulha, a U-shaped clay stove fed with wood, cow dung or crop waste, was used in most homes for centuries. Cooks sat on the floor, with a sil-batta [[@how-mixer-grinder|grinding stone]], clay pots and brass vessels within reach. Many Indian homes kept the kitchen as a special, clean space, and people often removed their shoes before entering.
